WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.090
- You look so beautiful.

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.240
- Thank you. You are too. Thank you.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.458
- Oh, my God!

00:00:05.458 --> 00:00:07.910
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:10.759
- Hello, everybody (chuckles).

00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:11.979
Oh, my goodness.

00:00:11.979 --> 00:00:15.540
- I'm still taking it in,
you look so beautiful.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.817
- Thank you. We had to pick a
special dress for you, Kelly.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.930
- Oh, my God! I was like, do
you look like this every day?

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.130
I hate you.
- No.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.170
- Oh, I was like, is this like what--

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.003
- Not at all (chuckles).

00:00:25.003 --> 00:00:26.940
- Well, I hear that you have
like a salon in your house,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.930
so you love getting ready.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.630
You love like hair and makeup, nails,

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:30.670
all that kind of stuff, right?
- I do.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.840
And I almost missed acting by one year.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.980
I didn't get accepted into the high school

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.710
of performing arts in DC, so I honestly--

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.363
- Who's laughing now high school?

00:00:42.384 --> 00:00:43.360
Who's laughing now?

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.470
- Well, I've since been back
to speak to the students.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.253
- Oh, never mind, we
love you. You're great.

00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:48.086
We great.
- It's okay.

00:00:48.086 --> 00:00:51.270
They love that story because, you know,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.090
a no doesn't mean it's the end.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.540
It was just a no from
that place, you know.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.320
But I had to learn that
at a very young age

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.360
and I didn't know--
- That's hard.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.820
- How to process it.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.470
So I thought that meant I couldn't act.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.400
So I went on

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.150
and I tried to enroll in
a school of cosmetology,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.440
but I needed to enroll in the ninth grade,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.020
and I was already into my 10th
grade year so I missed it.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.220
But it was always in me
because when I was in college,

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.860
I used to do $20 set acrylics.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.540
And I used to do--
- Wow! You can do acrylics?

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.410
- Yes. I taught myself. I
don't have a license, but

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:23.530
(audience laughs)

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.363
it's okay.

00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:26.580
- But it's great.
- It's fun.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.120
And I think I will credit that

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.950
to me being a creative person.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.930
I'm just very creative
and I like to create.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.313
- It's art, definitely.
- It really is.

00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:36.470
- I am not that kind of art.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.600
I am the opposite of you.
- Yes, you're (indistinct).

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.870
- I am like no makeup and jeans.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:41.890
I don't know if it's that either.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:42.854
I don't know.
- Yes you are.

00:01:42.854 --> 00:01:44.097
This is Kelly Clarkson.
- I don't know.

00:01:44.097 --> 00:01:44.930
- Come on.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:45.900
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:46.800
- But I have a friend who loves
her wigs and she names them.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.880
I actually started naming my weave.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.530
I started like, I have
a weave, a MacIntyre

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.350
and I have a weavy wonder.
- Oh, I love it.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.405
- Weavy necks. I started naming them all.

00:02:00.405 --> 00:02:03.600
And because it's funny,
and so she names hers.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.710
But you have a lot of wigs, right?

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.320
- I do.
- You name them, don't you?

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.410
- I just call them her, she, girl.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.890
- Oh!
- It's too many to name.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.410
I swear. I have wigs.
I've run out of wig heads.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.180
- I'm more personal, but whatever.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.047
I'm just kidding. I'm just kidding.

00:02:16.047 --> 00:02:18.650
- I be like, it's gon this girl today.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.250
Oh no, her. I'll do her.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.440
- It's kind of interesting
because it does kind of change

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.380
like whatever personality
you're having that day,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.283
or wherever you're at, you're
like, this is me today.

00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:27.500
- Yes.
- I love that.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.400
- And for me, a lot of
times, it's the color too,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.500
'cause I'll switch a color up
on you real quick (laughs).

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.850
- Yeah. Welcome back.
Taraji P. Henson is here.

00:02:34.752 --> 00:02:35.912
Give it up you-all.

00:02:35.912 --> 00:02:38.740
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.060
So I didn't know this, but
before you started acting,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.920
you worked at the Pentagon?

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.920
- Yeah.
- Like why? What?

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.360
- Why?
- What were you doing?

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.210
- Well, I kinda got there

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.240
because I had to pay off one university

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.710
so that I can enroll in Howard,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.990
and I owed $1000 before I
could get my transcript.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:56.980
So I needed a job.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.350
- No, I get it.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:02:59.183
I get that part, yeah.

00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:00.530
- And I'm from DC,

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.210
my entire family works
with the government.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.210
- Oh!
- So it's really nepotism.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:05.560
I was like, I called my step-mom.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.560
I was like, oh, do you
think you can give me a job?

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.910
She works at the Pentagon

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.280
and she got me into the
stay in school program.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.930
- Oh!
- Was a summer program,

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.080
but then once I paid off the,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.340
I didn't have quite
enough to pay the 1000 off

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.290
and summertime was coming to an end.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:22.520
So I went to

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:24.790
Henry Smith.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.130
She was a kick ass,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.254
can I say that?
- Sure.

00:03:27.254 --> 00:03:29.450
- Kick ass (chuckles) woman.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.790
And she ran my office.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:32.857
I don't remember exactly what she did,

00:03:32.857 --> 00:03:34.870
but she was very high up in the ranks.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:38.970
she was close to like a GS,

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.840
like a general in the service,
but she was a civilian.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.360
And I went to her and I was
just very honest with her.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.600
I was like, you know, I'm here,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.060
I'm trying to, you know,
pay off one university

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.420
so I can enroll at Howard University.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:54.253
And she heard me.

00:03:54.253 --> 00:03:56.350
And I was like, the summer program is over

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.760
and I heard you have a
stay in school program.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.340
And guess what she did,

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.320
she didn't have the program in her office

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.120
but she created a position for me.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.180
- What?
- Mm-hmm.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:06.013
- That's amazing.

00:04:06.013 --> 00:04:08.860
- That's how I was able to
go to school, to finish.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.660
- And you know what that
is, it's opportunity.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:12.210
Just giving some
opportunity and that chance,

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.090
it really did change this
trajectory of your life.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.380
- Lending a hand for someone
that you see might need.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.340
I mean, and it's always been
women, and it's been men too,

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.770
but I'm just saying like,
when women stick together,

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.360
we rise together.
- Absolutely.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:25.480
- Because when, thank you.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:26.860
- Instead of going threatening.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.990
- Exactly, instead of
going against each other,

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.970
because that's how society wants us to be,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:31.803
against each other.

00:04:31.803 --> 00:04:33.570
Because I think they know the powers

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.740
if we know that we are a
force to be reckoned with

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:38.140
when we sit together.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.171
No, seriously.
- Yes. No, agreed, agreed.

00:04:39.171 --> 00:04:40.930
- Because you know when I was in college,

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.210
I had become pregnant my junior year.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.030
Guess how I was able to finish,

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.810
Debbie Allen and Phylicia Rashad,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.050
they are both alumni of Howard University.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.353
- Yeah. They've both, yeah, yeah.

00:04:50.353 --> 00:04:53.870
- Yes. And they created a scholarship

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:55.720
in honor of their father, Andrew Allen.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.230
It was a triple thread
scholarship and I won it

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.990
and that's how I was
able to finish school.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:01.220
- Wow!

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.470
- Yes.
- That's so cool.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.250
- They're powerful women.
- They are incredible.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:07.350
- Well, speaking of school,

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:08.550
I didn't know this about you either,

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.720
but you were a teacher
before this? I didn't...

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.610
- Yes. I was--
- You're all these things.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.160
I'm like, what?
- Many many hats.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.960
- Yeah. I was like, that's crazy.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.220
- Well, when I came to LA
I didn't have a real plan,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.910
I just knew I needed to be
out here where the jobs were.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.900
And so I'm a single mom and
I have to make ends meet.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.560
And I just remember going, oh my God,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.310
Hollywood really doesn't care
about this degree in fine arts

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:34.143
that I have,

00:05:34.143 --> 00:05:37.060
so how else can I use this puppy?

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:38.940
You know, I'm like, broke,

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.370
trying to put myself through college.

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:43.110
And I figured it out, I
was substitute teaching.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.870
'Cause you could have
a degree in whatever.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:46.703
- Yeah.

00:05:46.703 --> 00:05:47.810
- But you needed a degree

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.570
to show that you finished,
you know, higher education.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:51.910
And so--
- You might as well use it.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:53.160
It costs a lot of money.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.060
- I had a whole group of us,
we were substitute teachers.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.350
- Wow!
- All of my girlfriends

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:58.183
in the struggle--
- That's hard.

00:05:58.183 --> 00:05:59.480
- It is hard.
- Kids can be mean.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.070
- I had a chair thrown
at me by a fourth grader

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.427
who called me the B word
and threw a chair at me.

00:06:04.427 --> 00:06:06.150
And I was like, throw another chair,

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.385
you go meet me at three o'clock outside.

00:06:08.385 --> 00:06:10.322
Like, I'm that kinda teacher.

00:06:10.322 --> 00:06:11.155
- I'm just saying,

00:06:11.155 --> 00:06:15.150
it happens everywhere, kids
are crazy at certain ages.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:18.130
But it's such a great thing
though to be able to do,

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.060
and to love kids and
have that kinda patience

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.831
to be able to do it, so
it's an honorable thing.

00:06:21.831 --> 00:06:23.570
- To be able to spark a young mind,

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:24.403
like that's a gift.
- That's cool.

00:06:24.403 --> 00:06:26.550
- Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.960
I gotta say, this will be
so interesting for you.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.720
You should like, you know,
write, produce or do something,

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:32.730
some kinda movie like this.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.310
This is so cool. This would
be a great, a great show.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.687
This would be a great thing to do.

00:06:36.687 --> 00:06:37.890
I would want to--
- You hear that, Hollywood?

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:38.837
- Yeah.

00:06:38.837 --> 00:06:41.280
- That script that I've been
working on for 10 years!

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.463
- Oh, my God, really? Oh, my gosh!

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:46.473
This is awesome,

00:06:46.473 --> 00:06:48.430
you've got Annie Live
airing tonight on NBC.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:51.750
- Like, do you get excited
about the live energy

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:52.950
or do you get nervous about it?

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.820
- Well, I was trained in live theater.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:58.550
I actually feel more
comfortable live on stage.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.340
- Me too, in theater, yeah.
- Because it's,

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:01.710
you know exactly what's,

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.310
if you hit the mark you know,

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.330
you know if you need to
put the foot on the gas

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.080
to get them involved, you know.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.847
But when you're on a set
is stagnant, like so--

00:07:08.847 --> 00:07:10.440
- And you have to repeat it

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.920
over and over.
- Yeah, but you have to wait.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.020
So you gotta wait till they yell, cut,

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.400
and then you go to dead land.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.180
And as soon as they yell,
cut, and they laugh,

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.280
you go, okay, I did
it. I did it, you know.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.980
But theater is like, they feed you,

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.100
you feed the audience, they feed you back.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:25.660
And there's a column response

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:28.980
that I can't really get
when you're on a set.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.450
- No. And every night in theater,

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:31.820
that's why I grew up in
musical theater as well,

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:32.653
it's different.

00:07:32.653 --> 00:07:34.610
You can have the same exact
show but it's so different,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.160
'cause the energy, like
where everybody's at,

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.313
the chemistry, I love live.

00:07:37.313 --> 00:07:39.650
- It's the only time in our business

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.130
where you get to live the story

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.020
from beginning, middle, to end.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:44.380
And you don't like--
- In the moment.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.590
- In the moment 'cause in
features and television

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:47.830
you all over.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.230
I remember Baby Boy,

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:50.910
'cause I was coming straight from theater,

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.660
I was like, what do you mean

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.330
we're shooting the last scene first?

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.780
- Oh, yeah. See that don't, yeah, no.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:58.613
How am I supposed to get there?

00:07:58.613 --> 00:07:59.530
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:01.050
- Yeah. But that's a skill

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:04.210
that I think being trained in theater

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.380
helps you to jump around like that,

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.650
because I know my script from
beginning, middle, and end

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:10.860
'cause that's how I was trained.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.220
Most people, not most,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.080
but some people that I
know that were not trained,

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.050
they will go through the script
and just find their stuff.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:18.313
- Oh, and they don't
look at the whole story--

00:08:18.313 --> 00:08:19.180
- You know what I mean?
- And how you react.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.560
Well then how can you be reactive

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:21.770
if you don't know what, yeah.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:23.290
- I don't judge. I'm just saying--

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.720
- I do.
- There's a different way.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:29.850
- Okay. So you play, so
you play Miss Hannigan now,

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.600
were you ever in any as a kid?

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.940
I feel like everybody did
something involving it as kid.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.920
- I sang The Sun Will Come Out Tomorrow

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.440
on my kindergarten graduation.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.510
- That's adorable.
- I still remember that at 51.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:42.053
That's when acting bomb
- I'm sorry,

00:08:42.053 --> 00:08:43.560
- Bit me.
- What?

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:44.670
- Yeah.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.430
- You're 51?

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:47.579
- Yeah. But you know I think...

00:08:47.579 --> 00:08:52.158
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:08:52.158 --> 00:08:53.505
- Don't you applaud that!

00:08:53.505 --> 00:08:55.696
- (laughs) Yes, applaud that!

00:08:55.696 --> 00:08:57.097
- I am offended!
- No.

00:08:57.097 --> 00:08:58.390
No, you know why?

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.970
I wanna be the representation for women

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:04.110
that your sexy, it never
dies until you're in the box.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.715
- Not everybody looks
like that at 51 (laughs).

00:09:06.715 --> 00:09:10.040
- But it's, listen,
it's how you feel about,

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:15.040
I decided that I wasn't
gonna allow the world, men,

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:18.160
or this industry, to
dictate how I live my life

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:19.210
and how I age.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:22.610
I'm going to turn 50
just like I turned 30,

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.150
except, you know, my knees--

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.684
- Are a little different.
- They're different.

00:09:25.684 --> 00:09:30.570
But, you know, I'm not going
to buy into my career is over,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.590
or life for me is over, or sexy is over,

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:35.210
or I shouldn't wear this,
I'm gon do what I feel.

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.150
- If you wouldn't have told me
that, I would've had no idea.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:38.983
- That's so fun.
- That's it.

00:09:38.983 --> 00:09:40.700
Because I can't believe it,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.030
I just looked up one day
and I was turning 50.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:45.740
I was like, oh, that how fast
it happens to these babies

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:46.573
out here.

00:09:46.573 --> 00:09:48.840
It happens that fast, like seriously,

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.462
and if you have kids--

00:09:50.462 --> 00:09:53.988
(Taraji and Kelly chattering)

